69} {:answer/id #uuid "285c3558-f151-51cd-8f5f-b1b41cc764e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db0a17e-67bc-5337-9db7-aa8831e48825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396469} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6eebb0b-3bd1-5fdc-aab2-c16f8bfe569f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ff91e14-b9a5-586b-b8da-fd89429ce569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the nonzero equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396472} {:answer/id #uuid "d16001f2-8334-5a4d-a5d3-2705ca6416c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the point of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396473} {:answer/id #uuid "a5776bca-8e30-5c52-81a1-7c0faeb65e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff91e14-b9a5-586b-b8da-fd89429ce569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the nonzero equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396472} :answer-field/id #uuid "af8b3b57-c322-5cc0-b2e2-876f370e51d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc687ab5-118b-5439-986b-20786139182f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334754" :question/problem "![](images/71/718df02f2d0c2f01cc4a02a40da120178afe0f436bbf38cd5a8d249d87b5d1c9.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d905c7de-54bb-580c-ac14-3568c1b81457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d905c7de-54bb-580c-ac14-3568c1b81457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370188} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0bd7840-6559-5707-b910-f2fb5c72d1d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69850b23-e61d-5814-b226-9ba564c143f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69850b23-e61d-5814-b226-9ba564c143f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370190} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c7dbd36-88be-5be0-b944-7943dca6b4c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "340e7604-73de-5dd2-91e0-923f082f78e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334999" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3xy=0 \\\\ 7y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3xy & =0 \\\\ x(-2+3y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{2}{3},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(7-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(7-5x) & =0 \\\\ 7-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{2}{3}).$"}]}
